[Music]  so my name is caroline hickman and i  want to talk to you about  talking about climate change and one of  the ways i want to do that  is by talking to climate change why  why do we want to do that well i think  one of the problems about climate change  is it's really really hard to imagine  if you're living in vanuatu living in  the maldives living in bangladesh  then we're seeing the immediate impact  of that but if you're living in europe  and you're living in the americas north  america in particular canada  then it is far from our immediate daily  concern  now if you're watching the television  you're online you're engaged you're  thinking about this  a lot which a lot of us are then we may  be thinking about this a great deal and  we might be having conversations with  each other about it  but a lot of people aren't and that's  one of the problems that we've got  in helping people start to engage with  climate change  now so what's our problem well one of  the problems  is that we've known about climate change  and we've known about what needed to be  done for decades  and we've not really acted on it we've  got technological solutions we've got  political  we've got economic arguments but we  still have a great  question mark about why we've not acted  on this  we've not got the same urgency around  climate and the biodiversity crisis as  we have about other  pandemic crises like the current virus  so why i'm a climate psychologist i  teach at the university  of balfour and i'm part of a group  called the climate psychology alliance  and what we want to do is bring a  psychological understanding  into thinking about why humans are not  responding with the urgency  that they need to to the climate and  biodiversity crisis  and yet of course some people are so  friday's for future greta tunberg  extinction rebellion  have been doing a fantastic job of  trying to change that story  and change that narrative but one of the  problems is is we get caught up  in a doom and gloom it's all terrible  it's all disastrous  argument versus oh no it'll be okay  the optimistic argument we can just  plant lots of trees or we can have  technological solutions  and we kind of bounce backwards and  forwards between the two  gus spare for climate scientists said  that he didn't think that technology was  the problem  with the climate change he thought that  we could easily find technological  solutions he said  that he thought the problems were  selfishness apathy  and greed now that's a very negative  approach to  human nature and human beings are  complex right we're very complex and  it's not  all destructiveness it's not all  negative and we've seen  with loving caring responses to  people around the world particularly at  the moment that actually things like a  crisis  globally can bring out both the worst in  people  but also maybe the best in people so  back to the psychology now i want to  tell you what  a child said so my research has been  primarily with children and young people  for five or six years now  and i want to tell you what one of the  children said when i started this  research because i think this would give  us some clues  i said to her well you know because i'm  a psychotherapist so it's not my  business to traumatize children and  terrified children  and yet i needed to find a way to talk  to them about frightening things  so obviously important one of the best  things to do with children and young  people is to ask them  one of the ways how can we talk with you  about this without scaring you  so i want to tell you what an  eight-year-old said to me she said well  she said you've got to tell me the truth  she said because if you don't tell me  the truth  about climate change she said you're  lying to me she said  and if you lie to me she said i won't  trust you  she said if i can't trust you then i  can't talk to you about things  i thought this was wisdom this was  genius from an eight-year-old  and then she followed it with and anyway  she said  i'm not a baby so part of the solution  in talking about climate change means  starting to uncover how we really feel  about it and it might also mean  that we have to search under the surface  of our barriers to climate change  we might have to accept the fact that  we're not entirely rational creatures  so first of all we want to go to  rational solutions about climate change  of course we do  and we want to talk about the science we  want to talk about the data and we want  to talk about  what would happen if we did this or we  did that  and i don't know about you but my head  spins when i look at the science  we are functionally psychologically  emotionally most of the time five  percent  conscious of what's going on now human  beings like to think that they're  rational creatures  but actually there's a heck of a lot  going on under the surface we're 95  unconscious i've got another image that  would help us think about  this we are largely unconscious so a lot  of what drives us  a lot of what makes us who we are as  humans a lot of  what would give us explanation about the  way we would approach things  particularly things that are threatening  and that's a risky is to look to the  unconscious mind  but when material from the unconscious  mind starts to bubble up into conscious  awareness  it scares us and it threatens us and we  defend against it  and that's when we hear the defenses  about climate change  we hear people go into climate denial  not what i think there's that much  climate denial around at the moment  i think for most people it's more of a  disavowal  what disavow looks like is on the one  hand you say yeah climate change is  really worrying it's really scary  and what are we going to do we've got to  do something particularly  when those images of the fires in  australia  and the destruction of the amazon  rainforest and the melting of the  glaciers  and the ice caps particularly when we  see that it brings into  conscious awareness and we start to feel  anxious and with there's been a lot of  discussion about eco anxiety and eco  grief  it pushes against our conscious  awareness and we get  really scared and we say yeah it's  really worrying  but then often we defend against that  with disavow and we say  oh it's really worrying but where are  you going on holiday this year  and oh but you know the government will  say this or science will say this or  technology will save us  something will happen to save us so we  move then into this sort of rescuer  fantasy  that either technology will save us or  economists will say this  one of the problems here is that there  is not a simple  solution searching outside yourselves  for the solution to climate change is  part of the problem  now part of the messaging has been well  we've got 12 years left to save the  planet it's less than that now of course  isn't it it's more like  10 or 9 or even less it's decreasing  because the messages we get all the time  is things are getting worse  it's hard to absorb this message it's  hard to make sense of it  we used to talk about it's five to  midnight actually now  groups of us are starting to say what if  it was five  past midnight what if it was too late to  actually  stop the worst impact of climate change  now i know  what that will do is trigger anxiety but  on the other hand if we don't start to  feel anxious we don't become aware  and if we don't become aware then we  don't care and if we don't care  then we don't take action so i want to  reframe this thing about this this  eco anxiety and start to call it eco  empathy that actually it's a sign that  we care  it's a sign that we're connected it's a  sign  of compassion and we need to move that  to a global empathy  so i've also been talking with children  in vanuatu the martial islands  bangladesh  and the maldives about this so my  question is is do we need to change this  story  and how can we change the story without  massively traumatizing people  because we need to take people with us  in this conversation  but we don't need to completely horrify  and overwhelm people  but on the other hand i'll tell you  what's a little bit more scary to me at  the moment  is this conversation about business as  usual  when we can go back to business when we  can get the economy  running again this is scary because  we are running out of time we need to  act and it's becoming  too late for billions of animals and  billions of people  so how do we find a way through this  well  back to the psychology so what i bring  to the table apart from  hopefully not scaring you too much i do  sometimes introduce myself in my talks  by saying  you know i am going to scare you but  stay with me to the end  because i'm going to show you what we  can do with some of that anxiety  what we need to do is get into those  feelings of course i'm going to say that  i'm a psychotherapist  but we need to know how to navigate  those feelings so that's some of the  work i've been doing  with artists and with storytellers and  with writers  and we've been doing that with children  and i want to show you a school project  that we did  so we wanted to talk with young children  and see if we could get them to imagine  climate change and we worked with a  group of children in wheelchair  and we worked with a artist friend of  mine called sonia shemaltsaday  now what sonja's been doing for years is  picking up sticks on beaches  and starting to draw and she does this  in a very imaginal creative  way and what she wanted to do was help  people start to see  and imagine the impact of something that  was too big to see  and this is what we think around climate  change it's almost too big to see  so it's too big to relate to it's what  timothy morton the philosopher calls a  hyper object  but what sonia does extraordinarily to  continue watching this video  click the link in the top left or in the  description below  or visit iai.tv for more debates and  talks from the world's leading thinkers  on today's biggest ideas

